{"type": "message", "id": "1c507336", "parentId": "119fb76f", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:35:54.908Z", "message": {"role": "system", "content": "", "sections": {"preamble": "You segment one lesion in a 3D MRI volume by operating an interactive segmentation model (nnInteractive) through `seg_*` tools. You cannot run code or read files; the tools are your only actions. Each tool returns text and, usually, images.\n\n## How pointing works\nYou never type coordinates. You point with **marks**: numbered labels drawn on the images.\n- Integer marks (e.g. `17`) are fixed 3D points spread over the body. Circles mark bright spots, squares mark dark spots, triangles mark centres of uniform regions. The same number refers to the same point in every view.\n- Marks starting with `E` (e.g. `E4`) lie on edges in the current image only. Use them as outline vertices.\n- All spatial arguments refer to the **current view**: the last image returned by `seg_view`. A mark is valid only if it is drawn in that view. When you use a mark, its position on the current slice is used.\n- A full-slice view shows coarse marks only. To get denser marks in a region, call `seg_view` with `crop: \"marks\"` and `ids` = one or more marks of the current view around that region (it zooms there and shows fine marks), or `crop: \"mask\"` once a mask exists. Use `marks: \"edges\"` to get edge marks for outlining.\n- Each `seg_view` returns two panels: left is the clean image, right has the marks. Compare them; labels can hide detail.\n\n## Workflow\n1. **Find the lesion.** `seg_open` gives the case card and an overview. Scan the volume with `seg_sheet` in at least two planes before choosing. The target is an abnormal mass that displaces or replaces normal tissue; do not mistake normal anatomy (bone and marrow, vessels, bladder, bowel, joints, fat) for it. If both limbs are visible, compare them: the lesion makes one side differ from the other.\n2. **Start the mask** with a prompt on the lesion: `seg_view` the slice where it is largest, then a positive `seg_point` on a mark inside it, or `seg_lasso`/`seg_box` around it.\n3. **Check and correct.** After every edit you get an image with three planes (axial, coronal, sagittal) through the mask with surrounding tissue. Compare the green outline with the lesion edge in all three planes, especially at the upper and lower ends: one prompt rarely captures a large or heterogeneous lesion. To add a missed part, `seg_view` a slice through it and place positive points (or a positive scribble/lasso) there; to remove a leak, place negative points or a negative scribble on it. `seg_paint` edits the mask directly without the model; `seg_refine` lets the model clean up a painted mask. `seg_edit` does component and morphology edits. `seg_undo`/`seg_checkout` restore earlier versions.\n4. **Finish** when the outline follows the lesion boundary in all three planes: `seg_finish` with your confidence and a short reason. The first `seg_finish` shows a final review (three slices per plane); inspect every panel, fix what is wrong, and call `seg_finish` again with the review code it prints to save.\n\nEvery edit returns: version id, volume, number of connected components, slice extent per plane, the change from the previous version, budget used, and the three-plane image (green = mask, red = removed).\n\n## Rules\n- Segment the whole lesion described in the task, and nothing else. If it is multifocal, include all parts.\n- Budgets: at most 40 tool calls and 15 model prompts (point/lasso/scribble/box/refine). Every call counts, including views and calls that fail. When the tool-call budget is used up, only `seg_finish` works. Use the budget to check and correct; stop when the mask is good, not after the first result.\n- Keep working until you call `seg_finish`. Do not stop to ask questions; nobody will answer.\n- Confidence in `seg_finish`: `high` only if the boundary is clearly correct in all three planes; `low` if you are unsure the mask covers the right structure.\n", "cwd": "<cwd>\n/home/ubuntu/agentic-segmentation/results/phase1-09-xhigh-ex/F50\n</cwd>"}, "timestamp": 1792370154907, "toolsAdded": [{"name": "seg_box", "description": "Run the model from a 2D box. Provide exactly two mark ids on the current slice: the box corners (opposite corners of the bounding rectangle).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["ids"], "properties": {"ids": {"type": "array", "items": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string"}]}, "description": "Exactly two mark ids: the box corners.", "minItems": 2, "maxItems": 2}}}}, {"name": "seg_checkout", "description": "Restore a specific mask version id reported in earlier observations (e.g. v3).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["version"], "properties": {"version": {"type": "string", "description": "Version id, e.g. v3."}}}}, {"name": "seg_edit", "description": "Non-model mask edit. keep_component/remove_component need a mark id inside the component. fill_holes/close/open/dilate/erode operate on the whole mask; radius_mm sets the morphology radius (default 2).", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["op"], "properties": {"op": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "keep_component"}, {"type": "string", "const": "remove_component"}, {"type": "string", "const": "fill_holes"}, {"type": "string", "const": "close"}, {"type": "string", "const": "open"}, {"type": "string", "const": "dilate"}, {"type": "string", "const": "erode"}]}, "mark": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string"}]}, "radius_mm": {"type": "number"}}}}, {"name": "seg_finish", "description": "Save the final mask and end the case. The first call (without review_code) shows a final review of the current mask (slices across each plane and just past the mask ends) and a review code. Check it, fix problems, then call seg_finish again with that review_code to save. Editing after the review requires a new review.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["confidence", "reason"], "properties": {"confidence": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "high"}, {"type": "string", "const": "medium"}, {"type": "string", "const": "low"}]}, "reason": {"type": "string", "description": "Short reason for the confidence."}, "review_code": {"type": "string", "description": "Code printed in the latest final review; required to save."}}}}, {"name": "seg_lasso", "description": "Run the model from a lasso: at least 3 ordered mark ids on the current slice form a closed polygon around (positive) or inside (negative) the lesion. Prefer this over many points for a rough outline.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["ids", "polarity"], "properties": {"ids": {"type": "array", "items": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string"}]}, "description": "At least 3 mark ids on the current slice, listed in order around the outline (clockwise or counter-clockwise); the polygon connects them in this order and closes back to the first.", "minItems": 3}, "polarity": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "positive"}, {"type": "string", "const": "negative"}]}}}}, {"name": "seg_open", "description": "Read the case card (volume shape, spacing, sequence, target text, mark convention, budgets) and get an orthogonal overview with orientation letters and a 10 mm scale bar. Call this first.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {}}}, {"name": "seg_paint", "description": "Edit the mask directly without the model: rasterize the polygon through ordered mark ids on the current slice and apply it to the slice range. mode add fills, mode erase removes. Use seg_refine afterwards to let the model clean the painted boundary.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["ids", "mode", "slice_range"], "properties": {"ids": {"type": "array", "items": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string"}]}, "description": "At least 3 mark ids on the current slice, listed in order around the outline (clockwise or counter-clockwise); the polygon connects them in this order and closes back to the first.", "minItems": 3}, "mode": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "add"}, {"type": "string", "const": "erase"}]}, "slice_range": {"type": "array", "items": {"type": "integer"}, "description": "Two slice indices [start, stop] on the current axis, both inclusive.", "minItems": 2, "maxItems": 2}}}}, {"name": "seg_point", "description": "Run the segmentation model from point prompt(s) on the current view. Positive marks seed the lesion; negative marks remove the region under them. Adds to the current mask.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["ids", "polarity"], "properties": {"ids": {"type": "array", "items": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string"}]}, "description": "Mark ids shown in the current seg_view, in order."}, "polarity": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "positive"}, {"type": "string", "const": "negative"}]}}}}, {"name": "seg_refine", "description": "Send the current mask to the model as an initial segmentation so it refines the boundary. Use after seg_paint or a rough lasso.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {}}}, {"name": "seg_scribble", "description": "Run the model from a scribble: ordered mark ids on the current slice form a polyline. Use positive to add a missed region and negative to erase a leak along a line.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["ids", "polarity"], "properties": {"ids": {"type": "array", "items": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string"}]}, "description": "At least 2 mark ids on the current slice, listed in the order the line should run; the polyline connects them in this order.", "minItems": 2}, "polarity": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "positive"}, {"type": "string", "const": "negative"}]}}}}, {"name": "seg_sheet", "description": "Contact sheet of evenly spaced slices along one axis, each labelled with its slice index. Use it to locate the lesion, then inspect single slices with seg_view.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["axis", "start", "stop", "n"], "properties": {"axis": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "axial"}, {"type": "string", "const": "coronal"}, {"type": "string", "const": "sagittal"}]}, "start": {"type": "integer", "description": "First slice index (inclusive)."}, "stop": {"type": "integer", "description": "Last slice index (inclusive)."}, "n": {"type": "integer", "description": "Number of slices to show."}}}}, {"name": "seg_undo", "description": "Restore the parent of the current mask version.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "properties": {}}}, {"name": "seg_view", "description": "Show one slice as a clean panel and a marked panel. Clean + marked are returned side by side. All mark ids refer to the view returned last. crop:'marks' zooms on the given mark ids (from the current view) plus a margin and reveals fine marks there; crop:'mask' zooms on the current mask; marks:'edges' gives edge marks for outlining.", "parameters": {"type": "object", "required": ["axis", "slice", "crop", "overlay", "marks"], "properties": {"axis": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "axial"}, {"type": "string", "const": "coronal"}, {"type": "string", "const": "sagittal"}]}, "slice": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string", "const": "mask"}]}, "crop": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "full"}, {"type": "string", "const": "mask"}, {"type": "string", "const": "marks"}]}, "overlay": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "none"}, {"type": "string", "const": "outline"}, {"type": "string", "const": "fill"}]}, "marks": {"anyOf": [{"type": "string", "const": "none"}, {"type": "string", "const": "coarse"}, {"type": "string", "const": "fine"}, {"type": "string", "const": "edges"}]}, "ids": {"type": "array", "items": {"anyOf": [{"type": "integer"}, {"type": "string"}]}, "description": "Required with crop:'marks': mark ids (int or \"E<n>\") shown in the current view to zoom on."}}}}]}}
{"type": "message", "id": "84bacc93", "parentId": "1c507336", "timestamp": "2026-10-19T00:35:54.908Z", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Case F50: segment the fibrous soft-tissue tumor on this T2-weighted MRI.\n\nVolume: 277x341x229 voxels, 1 mm isotropic. Budgets: max 40 tool calls / 15 model prompts / 10 min wall time.\nStart with `seg_open`. Finish with `seg_finish`.\n\n\nThe attached image is an example from a different patient (not this case): a fibrous soft-tissue tumor outlined in green in axial, coronal and sagittal slices. Use it to recognize this tumor type and to judge how much tissue the outline should include."}, {"type": "image", "omitted": true, "mimeType": "image/png", "sha256": "cc264c2afd68f2f6f555e6ccf24b691456783b18e8e48b48bf5ef208d586ead4", "bytes": 220460}], "timestamp": 1792370154907}}
